## 11. CRITICAL SQLITE CONCURRENCY AND DATA PROTECTION

Inspect how the existing application handles SQLite using sql.js or any other installed database engine.

Pay special attention to the risk of an in-memory database exporting a stale database snapshot over a file that has been changed by the external biometric collector.

Before every non-punch HRIS write operation:

- Acquire an appropriate central write lock.
- Ensure the latest external punch data is preserved.
- Execute the transaction safely.
- Commit to the actual production database.
- Verify the result.
- Release the lock.

Do not allow two conflicting HRIS write transactions to overwrite each other.

Use a safe single-writer or serialized transaction strategy.

Consider the existing SQLite transaction and journaling mode before changing it.

If a native on-disk SQLite driver would be materially safer or faster, assess the migration carefully.

Do not migrate simply for preference.

Any database migration requires:

- verified backup
- schema inspection
- data preservation
- migration testing
- rollback plan
- validation of external collector compatibility

Never drop or reset existing tables.

